{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: model systematically overpredicts levels vs realized prices, especially beyond ~day 7; apply a downward level shift that grows with horizon.",
    "Missed regime shift/mean reversion: when recent history is at/near local highs (uptrend into the forecast start), the model extrapolates the rally, but outcomes mean-revert lower; damp trend-following signals after strong 2–4 week run-ups.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following sharp rises in the last 5–10 days of history, forecasts remain elevated (80–85) while realized prices fall steadily (toward high-60s/low-70s); cap forecast upside and force faster decay back toward a medium-term mean.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: early horizon (days 1–5) errors are modest; mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors become large and increasingly negative (forecast minus actual). Use a horizon-increasing shrinkage toward a lower anchor (e.g., 20–30d trailing mean).",
    "Lag in capturing downtrends: once the realized path turns down, the model adjusts too slowly and keeps predicting flat-to-up; introduce a negative momentum correction when the last ~3–7 realized returns turn negative."
  ]
}
